WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.220
Attaching i-cord to the edge of a knitted
piece adds a nice finishing touch.

2
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.330
There are unlimited
variations to this technique.

3
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.420
Here are three for you to try.

4
00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:15.140
The first is plain i-cord along the edge.

5
00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.360
The second adds a few extra rows

6
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.740
in between and is a variation
of the worm trim that you may know.

7
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.860
I've heard the third
call a helicopter trim.

8
00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.220
Watch and see why this is
a very appropriate name.

9
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.100
Begin by casting on three stitches.

10
00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.780
Designer your machine
to slip in one direction.

11
00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:40.300
If you aren't familiar with this, look for
the Knit It Now video on knitting i-cord.

12
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.060
Pick up one loop of the knitted
garment and hang it on the end needle.

13
00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:50.680
This could be an edge stitch or a stitch
on the caston or bound off edge. Knit

14
00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:53.980
across and back,
slipping in one direction.

15
00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.340
Repeat this across the edge
of the piece you're finishing.

16
00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.780
Try this technique first on your
swatch to check the gauge.

17
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:04.850
But I generally use the garment
tension to knit an i-cord edge.

18
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.260
When you get to a corner,

19
00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:10.460
just knit a couple of extra rows
without picking up the garment stitch.

20
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:11.900
Keep picking up and knitting.

21
00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.960
Picking up and knitting and soon you'll

22
00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:19.080
see that you have a beautiful edge
that looks great on both sides.

23
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.780
The second i-cord edge is done the same

24
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.100
way, except you knit a few
rows between the pickup.

25
00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.620
In this example, we're knitting three rows

26
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:33.540
with six passes of the carriage
before picking up the next stitch.

27
00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.290
Pick up the next stitch or skip a few.

28
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.820
Try it both ways again.

29
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.060
Try this on a swatch.

30
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:45.160
It tends to flare and may not be
appropriate for your project.

31
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.660
The helicopter trim is done similarly
to the previous edge,

32
00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:00.660
but the trim is twisted before
you pick up the next stitch.

33
00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:05.180
As before, we're knitting three rows
with six passages of the carriage.

34
00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.460
Then turn the work for a few turns

35
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:10.500
and make sure the private side
of the work is facing you

36
00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.340
again.

37
00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.220
Then pick up the next stitch
of your knitted piece.

38
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.890
Repeat this across the row.

39
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.890
Again, you can pick up the next stitch
or skip a few rows as you can see.

40
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:28.880
See, the number of turns determines the
shape of the twisted i-cord that forms.

41
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.060
Mastering i-cord will go a long
way in creating your own designs.

42
00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.600
Play and have fun with them.

